<p>
In addition to carrying heavy teaching and administrative duties Dr. Hebard was unusually active in civic and public affairs.  She assumed leadership in World War work, naturalization and woman&apos;s suffrage.  She was active in preserving the unity of the University when decentralization was threatened.  She took the initiative in marking points of interest and commemorating many events important in Wyoming history.
</p>
